{
  "id": "Compound::DB00773 - Disease::DOID:10283",
  "verbalization": "Etoposide, a topoisomerase inhibitor, is linked to prostate cancer through its inclusion in the class of compounds that includes Mitoxantrone and Epirubicin, both of which are also topoisomerase inhibitors. The shared classification as topoisomerase inhibitors suggests a common mechanism of action, where these compounds interfere with DNA replication and repair by binding to topoisomerase enzymes, leading to DNA damage and cell death. This mechanism is consistent across the evidence, with all three compounds—Etoposide, Mitoxantrone, and Epirubicin—being classified under the same ontological category. The lowest common ancestor of these compounds, which is the organic cyclic compound and topoisomerase-II inhibitor, further supports the notion of a shared biological pathway. The evidence collectively implies that Etoposide may exert its anticancer effects through a similar mechanism, potentially offering a therapeutic avenue for prostate cancer treatment.",
